{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact head 73cb4f6f075de4d52184b4c4e13708e4049c5a07, resting on all 29 submitted artifacts, packages/log/README.md first.\n\nThe five findings in ea4a9bd0 are addressed: the synchronous owned cohort protects both pushed bytes and publisher state; explicit delegation kinds are judged at the grant; malformed JSON and field shapes produce structured prefix failures; retained files survive subsequent publication and reopen; and queued notification decisions use their target act's admission policy. The new tests cover positive cases as well as rejection cases. Two independent checks expose remaining false approvals.\n\n1. P2, expiry authority fails open on invalid timestamps. decode.ts checks entry.at and roster expiresAt only with str(); verify.ts passes Date.parse(e.at) to RosterReplay, which compares d.expiresMs \u003c= atMs. Either side can be NaN, so expiry is skipped. I constructed a fully signed valid grant expiring at entry 7: its delegated note at entry 7 correctly fails delegation-invalid; resealing only that entry with at:\"not-a-time\" makes verify report ok:true, verifiedThrough:7, failures:[]. A grant with expiresAt:\"not-a-time\" likewise admits a later delegated note and verifies. The same issue affects invitation expiry. Timestamp is RFC 3339 UTC with Z in the contract, and expiry is part of the promised authority verification. Reject invalid authority timestamps at the decoding boundary with a named failure and a prefix ending before the bad entry. Defend the replay API against non-finite time too, rather than treating it as unlimited authority. Add invalid entry time, invitation expiry and delegation expiry cases, keeping equality-at-expiry rejection and valid timestamps positive.\n\n2. P2, earlier published replay evidence is not verified. verify.ts 266 scans only top.files, although it reads all ancestor commits and claims to check the published history. I published genesis plus policy-activated without its retained policy: that head fails policy-missing. A normal child publication supplying the policy now reports ok:true for both commits. Separately, an earlier commit contains inputs/000...000.json with body \"{}\": it fails retained-digest alone; an advancing child removes that file, and verification again approves the entire two-commit history. No entry bytes or signatures were changed. A later commit must not erase a historical failure or retroactively make the earlier prefix replayable. Check retained-file digests and decoding in every inspected commit, and require the evidence needed for that commit's published entries to be present at that commit. Report the offending commit and preserve the accurate verified-prefix distinction. Reuse decoded content by digest if useful; do not require repeated policy evaluation merely to establish availability.\n\nArchitecture: the owned publication cohort and content-addressed retained-file union are sound improvements. The verifier remains independent of the Room, but approval of a history must account for evidence in its individual publications. Durable recovery of an unresolved publication remains a Room integration duty: retain the exact entries, checkpoint and retained-file cohort before I/O, retry the same commit, and record its checkpoint receipt once. This review does not waive the earlier lane A publication recovery finding.\n\nSecurity: explicit grants can no longer gain illegal kinds through a later promotion. Invalid times still bypass an authority boundary, and an incomplete or corrupt ancestor can disappear from the audit result. The reproductions use local signed fixtures, not a live attack or production credential. Fix both before relying on an offline verification success.\n\nSimplification: keep the single decoding boundary; give authority times one checked representation. Share digest-addressed decoding across commit views while checking availability and integrity per commit. There is no need to duplicate roster or policy execution for identical prefixes.\n\nValidation: clean dependency install; root workspace typecheck passed (the subsequent test command initially used an incorrect workspace name, then was corrected); unmodified log suite 57/57 Node and 52/52 workerd. Four independent diagnostic tests reproduced the two false-approval classes on both hosts, 4/4 each. Diagnostic file exists only in /tmp/artroom-checker-log-v2/packages/log/test/checker-v2-review.test.ts; no candidate source changed. Real local Git round trips and CLI malformed-input exit status are included in the passing suite. No live round-trip driver was executed.\n\nIntegration: amendment 2 is now approved and landed at main 5000a241. Preserve its required lane L edits (including onboarding-grant verification and policy/checker metadata) as explicit remaining work. Its R-LOG-10 admission-bound grant scope supports fixing \"*\" at grant time; reconcile the Room's current-role-only expansion before the integrated release. State re-derivation remains explicitly open under the approved contract, not implicitly claimed by this review. Schedule estimates do not reduce any of these obligations.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:24646837fb0e577a04e224e6b8ddb1ef42885ebe","binding":"assigned","head":"73cb4f6f075de4d52184b4c4e13708e4049c5a07","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:06b7cf94b6112f57893347661945836f0f94ebc6\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:26f088c3bc7090e81699ffd310739a4424989b87\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7fee2a7eb2ea9bd416051a21f41a38a700c1667c","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}